==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|eschete}}, from {{der|en|xno|escheat}}, {{der|en|fro|eschet}}, {{m|fro|escheit}}, {{m|fro|escheoit||that which falls to one}}, from the past participle of {{m|fro|escheoir||to fall}}, from {{der|en|VL.|*excadēre}}, from {{der|en|la|ex}} + {{m|la|cadō|cadere|t=fall}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əsˈt͡ʃiːt/}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːt|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|legal}} The [[return]] of [[property]] of a [[deceased]] person to the [[state]] (originally to a [[feudal]] [[lord]]) where there are no [[legal]] [[heir]]s or [[claimant]]s.
# {{lb|en|legal}} The property so [[revert]]ed.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[plunder|Plunder]], [[booty]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|III|viii|text=Approching, with bold words and bitter threat, / Bad that same boaster, as he mote, on high / To leaue to him that Lady for '''excheat''', / Or bide him battell without further treat.}}
# That which falls to one; a reversion or return.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=V|stanza=25|passage=And by my ruines thinkes to make them great: / To make one great by others losse, is bad '''excheat'''}}

